```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            // Previous array list
            int old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int old_indexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int old_lastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Insert the item
            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            // New state of array list
            int new_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int new_indexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int new_lastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool new_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Verify that size has increased by 1
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old_arrList_Count", old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new_arrList_Count", new_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(new_arrListCount == old_arrListCount + 1);
            // Assert that the index and last index of the inserted item have not changed
            Debug.Assert(old_indexOfX == new_indexOfX);
            Debug.Assert(old_lastIndexOfX == new_lastIndexOfX);
            // Assert that the Contains property of the list should not change
            Debug.Assert(old_arrListContainsX == new_arrListContainsX);
            // Assert that the index of the item in the list is either its old value or the next highest after insertion
            Debug.Assert(new_indexOfX == index || new_indexOfX == old_indexOfX + 1);
        }
    }
}

```

This new C# code performs two functions: it adds instrumentation to validate that the `ArrayList`'s size increases by 1 after an insertion operation, as well as that other properties of the `ArrayList` remain unaltered.--------------------Here are the matching Debug.Assert statements:

```cs
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);
```

In the provided program, the assertion checks whether the count after the insertion operation is one more than the count before the insertion operation. This is aligned with the requirement specified in the corresponding comment, that the size of array list should increase by 1, after a successful insertion.--------------------```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            Debug.Assert(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0, "[PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList index out of range.");

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount + 1 == New_arrListCount, "ArrayList size increased by 1 after insertion.");
            Debug.Assert(Old_index == New_arrListIndexOfX, "The inserted item was placed at correct index.");
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == IndexOf(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, x), "The last occurrence of the inserted item is its correct position.");
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX, "The ArrayList contains the inserted item after insertion.");
        }
    }
}

```
In above code, we have added debug assertions according to the comments. 

Assert.AreEqual and Assert.AreSame methods are generally used in unit tests to make sure that the methods are working as expected. But these do not automatically provide a test failure if the condition is false. Instead, they work as reminders/guidelines to ensure your code is functioning as it should.

For unit testing, you use Debug.Assert for this kind of functionality which actually fails the test (or method in which the Debug.Assert lives) if the condition being tested is false. It's just like a normal Debug.Assert statement, but will only execute when running as a Debug build, not Release. It won't stop the program from running, but it will generate a message in the Output window and event log.--------------------The following code will match any `Debug.Assert` statements in the given code. Please be aware this is not actually the assert statement. Assert is used to ensure that the specified condition is true at this specific moment. There are several `Assert` statements in the given code snippet but because we are matching the pattern `Debug.Assert\(.*?\);`, which is basically matching any `Debug.Assert` statement, therefore you will get all of these in the output. If you need this to be specific, please provide more detail.

